the Super Nintendo is my first console  it shaped the way I think and feel about  videogames today from its deep RPGs to  lengthy action adventures so needless to  say that revisiting this platform in its  games sent me on field trips which sets  the SNES classic apart from the NES  classic is inherent what the platform  offered in its era took me from 8-bit to  16-bit open the door for games would be  much more expansive and nuanced this is  what propels the SNES Classic from  simply being a novelty first let's look  at what you get the console itself two  controllers a five-foot HDMI cable a  five-foot USB mini cable to power the  system a USB power brick and a little  treat an old-school folded poster not to  mention the 21 games pre-loaded onto the  system all for 80 bucks the SNES classic  looks identical to the original that  released in North America but much  smaller and lighter the power button  function is just the same and the reset  switch brings you back to the main menu  it's actually the only way to get back  to the home screen and it's kind of  annoying if your setup isn't within  reach eject and the cartridge slot are  just for show since aren't any  cartridges to insert the front panel may  look like the original controller ports  but it pops outward and stays attached  the controllers themselves are identical  to the original SNES and the buttons  have the same solid tactile feel a  downside is that the d-pad feels a bit  stiff compared to modern game pads one  thing you don't have to worry about this  time around is coeur d'alene at about 5  feet it's twice as long as the NES  classic controllers at the back of the  console you'll find the meaning USB port  for power and HDMI out this little thing  doesn't need much power with its 5 volts  spec and can even be powered through a  USB port built into TVs or a computer  saving you an outlet the SNES classic  upscales games from their 240p  resolution to 720p a multiple of the  original resolution native 1080p would  have resulted in slight pixel Distortion  now let's fire this thing up the system  boots straight into the snappy main menu  options are up top where you can  customize the visuals want fake scan  lines from the days of CRTs traditional  four by three or perfectly square pixels  it's up to you and here you can choose  the type of border you want to fill  your widescreen display since all games  run as they did back in the day if you  turn on my gameplay demo which uses your  own gameplay footage or classic den  while using Filton footage Mario will  walk by and hit a game block from the  main menu if you leave your system idle  for a minute it'll start to roll  gameplay footage in the background it's  cute just like the lovely menu music the  suspended point system returns from the  NES classic you could stop and resume a  game at any point the tricky part is  games with internal saves can have the  data point overwritten if you enter an  earlier suspend point now the reason why  you'd want an SNES classic is to revisit  the greatest hits from Nintendo 16-bit  libraries or experience what you might  have missed all in one packaged not to  disparage the 8-bit era but what  separates the SNES from the NES is that  the extra power afforded developers the  opportunity to create greater games more  expressive visuals for stronger  characterizations and game worlds deeper  stories longer adventures and more  nuanced mechanics and some of the best  soundtracks in all of gaming  I genuinely enjoyed pouring hours into  Super Mario RPG again from it's  unforgettable songs to unprecedented  story beats in the Mario universe it's  truly unique and what other game will  you have Bowser peach and Mario fighting  alongside each other it was an  achievement in world building and  ushering in a new wave of RPG fans Final  Fantasy 3 or better known as Final  Fantasy 6 opened a massive world to us  with 40 plus hours of role-playing  giving you every reason to gather party  members learned their roles and  abilities and take down kefka and his  evil empire movements a bit slow and  random encounters are dated but  unraveling Terra's story and all of its  branches still has a place in 2017  another tremendous RPG and Secret of  Mana deserves a full playthrough I want  to get a hundred percent in Super  Metroid a sterling example of the  metroidvania genre we still see today we  even get a real release of Star Fox 2  and while it's a game that hasn't aged  well it's neat that the SNES classic  offers something exclusive I haven't  even mentioned Super Mario World Legend  of Zelda linked to the past or  earthbound many of these games are  realizations of ideas set in motion from  the NES era and transcend their Hardware  limitations of their time speaking of  hardware that emulation delivers an  authentic SNES experience I spent time  with all 21 titles to get a feel for how  they ran by and large the games look  true to form and run exactly as you  would expect for better and worse which  also means a few games suffer from  framerate drops Street Fighter 2 turbo  had some slowdown with characters  pulling off flashy attacks Megaman X did  as well so the system is mimicking the  performance of the real Hardware  adhering frame rates of the original  cycles of these games it's not game  breaking but it is noticeable and  undermined a few moments the SNES  classic is a closed system what you see  is what you get  barring a hypothetical firmware update  or expansion and wouldn't have been nice  to see Chrono Trigger or Final Fantasy 2  or Final Fantasy for sure but that  doesn't negate the fact that we still  have an all-star roster yes I'm aware  that there are so many ways to play  these games and we've had several  versions of them through the years but  to have this lineup of iconic games in  one place in an easy accessible and  convenient fashion makes the SNES  classic worth seeking out if Nintendo is  looking to capitalize on  16-bit nostalgia I'd say they did a damn  fine job  [Music]
 

